{"record":"parent_entry","position":{"parent_record":"request:00000000000000000374:0000","subposition":null},"original_role":"assistant","source":"assistant_message","trust":"untrusted","provenance":null,"content":"{"kind":"assistant_message","text":"Committed locally, but I can't push from here — two strikes, stopping per your rule:\n\n1. `git push -u origin feature/feature-flags` → `fatal: unable to access 'https://github.com/drosemann/infra-pilot.git/': schannel: AcquireCredentialsHandle failed` — no credentials for GitHub in this environment.\n2. No `gh` CLI installed to try an alternative route.\n\n**Done:** commit `a164220` on new branch `feature/feature-flags` (34 files, +1537/−3, with your `Co-authored-by: Muse Code <muse-code@meta.com>` trailer). Working tree is clean.\n\n**You run:** `git push -u origin feature/feature-flags`, then open a PR with the text below — written to be fun to read, all facts verified from the actual diff:\n\n---\n\n# 🚩 Feature Flags: a kill switch for every \"oops\" (panel → orchestrator → CLI → Discord)\n\n## TL;DR\n\nInfra-pilot can now turn features on and off **without a redeploy, without a SaaS vendor, and without begging the pipeline for forgiveness**. One admin UI, one Postgres source of truth, four consumers, one shared brain (a YAML contract both the TypeScript and Python evaluators swear allegiance to). Rolled out behind `FEATURE_FLAGS_ENABLED=false`, so this whole PR changes exactly **nothing** until you opt in. You're welcome, production. 💅\n\n## 🎬 The plot\n\nOnce upon a time, every feature in infra-pilot was either ON (deployed, good luck) or OFF (a code change, a rebuild, a prayer). There was no middle ground, no \"let's try this on 10% of orgs,\" no big red button for 3 AM. This PR fixes that with a self-hosted flag system built on the Postgres and Redis you already run — no LaunchDarkly phone-home, no new sidecar to babysit.\n\n## 🗺️ What's inside\n\n- **🗄️ Source of truth (panel):** `feature_flags` + `feature_flag_rules` tables ([migration](C:\\Users\\danie\\OneDrive\\github-repos\\infra-pilot\\services\\management-panel\\db\\migrations\\001_feature_flags.sql)), per-environment enable / 0–100% rollout / org+project+user targeting / deny-lists / expiry. Seeded with `living_fleet` and `discord_service_v2`, both born OFF, as nature intended.\n- **🔌 API:** `GET /api/feature-flags/evaluate` returns only *resolved values* (targeting rules never leave the server — your allow-lists aren't bedtime reading for browsers). Writes are admin-only, audit-logged, and every write fires a **WebSocket nudge** (`?channel=feature-flags`) so the UI updates without a reload. Service-to-service auth via `FEATURE_FLAGS_SERVICE_TOKEN`, fail-closed like its federation sibling.\n- **🖥️ UI:** `/feature-flags` admin page with enable toggles, rollout sliders, and a **Kill button with a confirm dialog**, because the best buttons are the scary ones.\n- **🐍 Orchestrator:** stdlib-only `feature_flags.py` (cache → env override → safe default, never raises), gating `living_fleet`, plus new `feature-flag:read/manage` RBAC permissions.\n- **⌨️ CLI:** `ipilot flags list|evaluate|create|rollout|kill` — yes, you can now kill a feature from a terminal at 3 AM. Living the dream.\n- **🤖 Discord:** `feature-flags.js`, same semantics, degrades to \"feature off\" if the panel is unreachable (the bot stays up; the feature just naps).\n- **🧹 Hygiene:** `scripts/flag-hygiene.py` snitches on expired or 90-day-untouched flags, with `--fail-on-stale` for CI shaming.\n\n## 🧪 Tests (written, currently grounded)\n\nNew suites: panel unit + integration, CLI commands + client URL assertions, orchestrator contract tests **loaded from the shared YAML fixture**, hygiene-script tests. Full honesty: this environment has no working Node or Python, so nothing was executed here — CI gets to be the hero. One test already earned its keep pre-flight: it caught the CLI client double-prefixing `/api/api/feature-flags`. Fixed. The test stays as a